@node introduction
@chapter Introduction

@menu
* Beyond this Manual: beyond this manual.
* Highlights: highlights.
@end menu

Steel Bank Common Lisp (SBCL) is a free and open-source ANSI Common
Lisp implementation with a high-performance native @ref{compiler}. In
addition, it provides @ref{unicode support}, @ref{threading}, an interactive
environment including a @ref{debugger}, a @ref{statistical profiler}, a code
coverage tool, and many other extensions.

@itemize
@item Homepage: @url{http://sbcl.org}

@item @ref{reporting bugs}: @url{https://bugs.launchpad.net/sbcl}

@item Mailing lists: @url{https://sourceforge.net/p/sbcl/mailman/}

@item Repository: @url{https://sourceforge.net/p/sbcl/sbcl/}

@item GitHub mirror: @url{https://github.com/sbcl/sbcl}

@item Supported platforms: @url{https://www.sbcl.org/platform-table.html}
@end itemize

This manual focuses on behavior specific to SBCL, not on behavior
common to all implementations of ANSI Common Lisp. See
@ref{further resources} for information about @ref{the sbcl project} and
Common Lisp.

@node beyond this manual
@section Beyond this Manual

Documentation for non-ANSI extensions for various commands is
available interactively. Tools that have their own command
prompts (e.g. the @ref{debugger}, and @code{inspect}) provide a @code{help} command.
Extensions without a command prompt (such as @code{trace}) are described in
their @code{documentation} strings, unless your SBCL was compiled with the
@code{--without-sb-doc} option to omit them (see @ref{customizing the build}),
in which case they are only readable in the source code.

This manual itself is available interactively via the @ref{sb manual}
module.

Besides this user manual, the following files are included in
@ref{source distribution}s, @ref{binary distribution}s and the
@ref{sbcl repository}. When SBCL is installed, they
may be found in e.g. @code{/usr/local/share/doc/sbcl/} or
@code{/usr/share/doc/sbcl/}.

@itemize
@item @code{COPYING}: License and copyright summary

@item @code{CREDITS}: Authorship information on various parts of SBCL

@item @code{NEWS}: Summaries of changes between various SBCL versions
@end itemize

And, of course, don't forget the @ref{language reference}.

@node highlights
@section Highlights

SBCL comes with numerous extensions, some in core and some in modules
loadable with @code{require}. We only list some of the highlights here.
Unfortunately, not all of these extensions have proper documentation
yet.

@itemize
@item @strong{Foreign Function Interface:} The @code{sb-alien} package allows
  interfacing with C-code, loading shared object files, etc. See
  @ref{foreign function interface}.

@ref{sb grovel} can be used to partially automate generation of
foreign function interface definitions.

@item @strong{Native Threads:} SBCL has native threads on numerous platforms,
  capable of taking advantage of SMP on multiprocessor machines. See
  @ref{threading}.

@item @strong{Network Interface:} The @code{sb-bsd-sockets} module is a low-level
  networking interface, providing both TCP and UDP sockets. See
  @ref{networking}.

@item @strong{Operating System Interface:} The @code{sb-ext} package contains a
  number of functions for running external processes, accessing
  environment variables, etc.

The @ref{sb posix} module provides a lispy interface to standard
POSIX facilities.

@item @strong{Profiling:} The @code{sb-profile} package provides an exact,
  per-function @ref{deterministic profiler}, while the @code{sb-sprof} module
  is SBCL's @ref{statistical profiler}, capable of call-graph generation
  and instruction level profiling, which also supports allocation
  profiling.

@item @strong{Executable Delivery:} The @code{:executable} argument to
  @code{sb-ext:save-lisp-and-die} can produce a stand-alone executable
  containing both an image of the current Lisp session and an SBCL
  runtime.

@item @strong{Timeouts and Deadlines:} SBCL allows restricting the execution
  time of individual operations or parts of a computation using
  @code{:timeout} arguments to certain blocking operations, synchronous
  timeouts and asynchronous timeouts. The latter two affect operations
  without explicit timeout support (such as standard functions and
  macros). See @ref{timeouts and deadlines}.

@item @strong{Metaobject Protocol:} The @code{sb-mop} package provides an
  implementation of the metaobject protocol for the Common Lisp
  Object System as described in @emph{The Art of the Metaobject Protocol}
  by Kiczales et al.

@item @strong{Extensible Sequences:} SBCL allows users to define subclasses
  of the @code{sequence} class. See @ref{extensible sequences}.

@item @strong{Extensible Streams:} The package @code{sb-gray} provides an
  implementation of @ref{gray streams}.

The @ref{sb simple streams} module is an implementation of the Simple
Streams API proposed by Franz Inc.

@item @strong{CLTL2 Compatibility Layer:} The @code{sb-cltl2} module provides
  @code{sb-cltl2:compiler-let} and environment access functionality
  described in @emph{Common Lisp The Language, 2nd Edition} which were
  removed from the language during the ANSI standardization process.

@item @strong{Recursive Event Loop:} SBCL provides a recursive event
  loop (@code{serve-event}) for doing non-blocking IO on multiple streams
  without using threads.

@item @strong{System Definition Tool:}
  @uref{https://www.sbcl.org/manual/asdf.html, ASDF} is a flexible and
  popular protocol-oriented system definition tool by Daniel Barlow.
  It is bundled with SBCL. Load it with @code{(require :asdf)}.
@end itemize

